00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
the brexit talks between labour and the  government are expected to continue  tomorrow  in an attempt to try and break the  impasse and joining us now is the shadow  attorney general baroness shami  chakarbati thank you very much for being  on the programme this morning now very  keen to talk to you about brexit but i  did just want to start with the  allegations that are in the sunday times  about the way that labour is dealing  with complaints of anti-semitism within  the party  um according to the sunday times labour  members investigating  investigated for posting harl hitler  dues the problem  haven't been expelled even though the  complaints were received a year ago  and jeremy corwin's office involved in  approving delaying or blocking  at least 101 complaints of anti-semitism  i mean it's nearly three years since  your report  what's your reaction to this well i have  to say i you can imagine that i checked  this out before coming on because i  fully expected to  you know to to have to talk about this  um  the part those people in the party who  have looked at the specific allegations  in the sunday times refute then  and they actually challenged the  accuracy of the reporting some of the  emails that have been leaked  actually demonstrate the opposite of  what is being spun in the sunday times  that's what my colleagues tell me that  particular lines are extracted from  emails  to actually give the opposite impression  of what was the fact  so that often some of these emails are  about actually disciplining people  and they're quoted out of context to  make it look like the reverse but but  there is a serious issue  here um we have had a problem in the  labour party and by the way it goes  way back i um i looked at a very  unsavory  general election poster whilst i was um  waiting to come on from 2005  presenting michael howard in a in a very  unattractive way  that i think can only be described as an  anti-semitic trope that was 2005.  let's be honest we've had problems as as  there are in all parties not just on  anti-semitism but other forms of racism  too  we sort of heard that from sam jima a  few minutes ago  we have to not be partisan about  tackling racism we have to not be  factional  there was a period between general  secretaries when um  and then some junior staff who were  dealing with complaints  looked to the leader's office for for  leadership  and advice and support but that practice  ended  when jenny formby uh took over  that's a big deal for a lot of people  isn't it like an interesting example  so just to be clear on that because i  have quizzed people about this  because i've got a personal investment  in this as you know as well as coming on  to your to your show there was a hiatus  between general secretaries  when junior staff who were dealing with  complaints um  asked for leadership and guidance they  had the final decision any of that's  still going on  i am told unequivocally no because  because jenny formby is a very strong  general secretary who is committed to  making this  a priority so there is no interference  from jeremy corbyn  or his staff in the disciplinary process  now does that mean  that we're yet where we want to be no of  course not because  one one example of bad behavior is one  too many but we've been trying to  improve our processes  speed them up at the same time you have  to speed them up and still be robust and  fair  so there's more to do but um but there  is no  interference from jeremy or his staff  that under the new general secretary  we've we've got a a more correct process  i mean you are  one of the uk's leading human rights  advocates  aren't you really angry about this i am  very angry but but  but i returned to this point whether  it's islamophobia in the conservative  party or other forms of racism as in sam  jima's case we're talking about whether  or not no  no no no absolutely i don't believe  that you can tackle racism in any party  or in society if you make it a party  political or faction or internal  factional  matter i think we have to just say that  this is too  important to politicize or factionalize  we have  problems we have had problems for some  time  in the labour party and there's a  particular manifestation  of anti-semitism that you sometimes get  on the left  which is to confuse it with anti-elitism  say tropes about bankers and can  you know that's a particular danger and  trap that  that is there on the left we have to  tackle it  but it will be much easier to tackle it  if we don't make it  um a personal attack on jeremy corbyn  or a or a conservative attack on labor  or  or an inter-factional attack it seems to  me and we we need to take the same  approach by the way  when there are claims about the  conservative party not go oh the tories  have got a problem with islamophobia now  we've got to come together as democratic  people  in a context of the rise of the far  right  in europe and in this country the  democratic people who are anti-racist  need to come together  and tackle it in that way i mean later  today the jewish labour  movement are going to be voting on  whether they have confidence in jeremy  corbyn's leadership so my plea to my  plea to then  is is not to personalize it  because as i say that post feel that  it's okay  look of course i understand that and  he's the leader of the party and that's  what they would say  that how do how can we how can we take  on our party  without a vote of confidence in the  leader my plea  to the jewish labor movement is to stay  in the labour movement  and to tackle racism together not  to personalize it and make it about  jeremy corbyn because he is one person  and he won't be leader  forever we have to make this  non-factional  non-personal and and work together and  that's that's my plea  to the colleagues and comrades in the  jewish labour movement who  are such an important part of labour's  history and labour's story  okay um now i'm keen to move on to  brexit and jeremy corbyn and theresa may  have been  having talks last week we've got more  talks planned for tomorrow  are you optimistic that there's the deal  to be struck  i'm not i'm one of life's optimists so  you know i always try to be  uh optimistic but i did speak um to  people who've been  in the room i spoke in particular uh  last night to rebecca long bailey  our shadow business secretary and so far  um our  impression is that mrs may has not moved  an  inch on her red lines that is worrying  to me  because the clock is running down we're  sort of  almost beyond midnight because you know  with past the 29th  of march um she's asking for more time  but but you have to compromise and  compromise is not just telling us again  and again how good your existing deal is  that you know this is a deal that's been  voted down in parliament  three times and my understanding is that  whilst people have been  mutually respectful in these discussions  um you know they've been they've been  respectful and friendly and positive in  tone  in substance as yet there's been not  a jot of movement from the government  and that just  that isn't going to break the deadlock  i'm afraid i'm interested to know if  there's been any compromise on labour's  uh side as well uh particularly with  regards to a second referendum because  we've been hearing slightly different  things from  the shadow cabinet we can have a quick  look just to illustrate to our viewers i  thought you might do that  exactly and just to make it clear we've  got uh emily thornbury the shadow  foreign secretary saying any deal agreed  by parliament  must be subject to a confirmatory public  vote and rebecca long bailey on the  other side  if we get exactly what we want a good  strong deal then i would struggle to  find a reason to put that to a public  vote um i mean you can see why people  are confused is it a red line or  what is the position so you um know that  as of our party conference last autumn  um a second referendum became part of  our policy but as sam gina said it's a  process point it's not an end in itself  and the purpose of having a second  referendum would be  to stop a catastrophic no deal that we  cannot countenance for the reasons that  that just phillips said in in that  fantastic interview by the way it was a  wonderful piece of political tv  we cannot have a no deal because it  would be devastating to the lives  of so many people in britain to business  to the trade unions  but in particular to the poor so to stop  a no deal to stop a bad deal and  crucially to break a parliamentary  deadlock  i have to more and more agree with sam  and with jess that we can't go on like  this if we can't get  proper proper real consensus in  parliament it seems to me  that a public vote that includes a  general election or a second referendum  become more and more likely even  necessary but it's not a red line  if just listening to you if jeremy  corbyn and theresa may do manage to get  a deal that they can both agree on  that wouldn't necessarily have to be put  to a public focus well i'm i'm  personally cautious about you know  putting shammy's red lines out when  shammy's not in the room and we're  saying we're in the compromise business  but i'm telling you what i think is  likely i don't see  at this point in this process after  nearly three years since the referendum  with no um no agreement in the  conservative policy  with um yes we some of the options that  are closer to our preference like a  customs union  um got the most support in parliament  but that still was not  overwhelming support i i find it almost  impossible to  to imagine that theresa may and jeremy  corbyn can go and have a cup of tea in a  room  or several cups of tea over several days  and then come out  and the smoke goes up and everyone's  going to be happy so it seems to me this  has been left  so late in the day and i'm afraid i must  criticize mrs may a bit for that  it's been left so late in the day it's  hard to imagine  that we're going to make real progress  now without either a general election  or as or a second referendum on any deal  that she can get over the line in  parliament and it has to be  got over the line in parliament this is  not going to be a bilateral  a bilateral deal between the leaders of  the  of the two main parties they need to be  talking about what might get through the  house of commons i mean talking about  areas of agreement the prime minister  said this morning that the fact is on  brexit there are  areas where the two main parties agree  the first example she gives  is we both want to end free movement do  you share the same policy on immigration  free movement well clearly i mean she  and her party are a much more  anti-immigration party and labor's never  been an anti-immigration party there  have been times in the past when i  haven't liked some of the labour party's  rhetoric let's be clear  on immigration i don't think that helped  when those same people  were were so uh campaigning for remain  um in the referendum however um we are  where we are at our in our manifesto  in 2017 we said when you leave the eu  the concept of free movement ends but  that doesn't mean movement ends  so i do think that we are much more  positive about  the rights of eu nationals in the uk and  indeed of brits in  europe and and movement between the two

